Corneal Abrasion
Opthalmology & ENT
Acute severe eye pain, Foreign body sensation, Fluorescein-staining defect

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-015
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, blinding eye pain and inability to open the eye following a minor traumatic incident.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage detailed acute, severe, sharp unilateral eye pain, relentless reflex tearing, and severe photophobia immediately following a focal trauma (child's fingernail). This presentation required an urgent, immediate home assessment to exclude a full-thickness penetrating globe injury, identify retained foreign bodies, and definitively diagnose a corneal epithelial defect. Immediate pharmacological intervention was required to break the severe pain cycle and initiate prophylactic antibiotics prior to routine hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My baby poked me in the eye, and now it feels like there is a shard of glass under my eyelid. I can't even pry it open."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports acute, severe, sharp right eye pain that commenced immediately after their infant accidentally scratched their eye 2 hours ago. Experiences profuse, uncontrollable reflex tearing and epiphora. Endorses severe photophobia causing significant distress in the lit room. Reports a pronounced foreign body sensation physically preventing voluntary eye opening (reflex blepharospasm). Denies visual loss prior to the injury or discharge of fluid from the eye.
Functional Status: Currently incapacitated by intense pain and inability to open the right eye.
Social/Safety Concerns: Distressed, unable to assist with childcare due to pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: In obvious distress, head bowed, holding a towel over the right eye.
Physical Exam: Instillation of one drop of topical Tetracaine 1% immediately relieved the blepharospasm, allowing full examination. Visual acuity in the right eye is 6/9 (mildly blurred secondary to tearing). Diffuse bulbar conjunctival injection and pronounced ciliary flush are present. Eversion of the upper eyelid reveals no retained subtarsal foreign body. Cornea appears clear to gross inspection, but fluorescein staining under a cobalt blue light reveals a distinct, brilliantly fluorescing, linear, well-demarcated 3mm epithelial defect in the central cornea. Anterior chamber is deep and quiet; no hyphema or hypopyon. Pupils are round, equal, and reactive.
Environmental Observations: Dimly lit room at patient request.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Traumatic Corneal Abrasion - Status: Acute
Differentials:

Penetrating Globe Injury - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal, deep anterior chamber (no shallowing), round/reactive pupil (no teardrop pupil), and no prolapse of uveal tissue or extruding intraocular contents.

Retained Subtarsal Foreign Body - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete direct visualization of the everted upper tarsal conjunctiva revealing no particulate matter.

Infectious Corneal Ulcer - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Immediate traumatic onset, linear non-infiltrated defect on fluorescein, and absence of a dense, white, opaque stromal opacity.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe, incapacitating eye pain following focal trauma. The immediate clinical priority was to absolutely rule out a catastrophic penetrating open-globe injury, which was successfully excluded via careful physical examination after localized topical anesthesia. The use of fluorescein staining definitively confirmed a traumatic corneal abrasion. By diagnosing the abrasion and ruling out retained foreign material or perforation, the urgent visit prevented a painful, blind transfer to the emergency department, allowing for immediate initiation of prophylactic topical antibiotics and systemic analgesia to ensure a safe, localized recovery overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Chloramphenicol 1% eye ointment QID for 5 days for bacterial prophylaxis. Prescribed oral Paracetamol 1g QID and Ibuprofen 400mg TDS for systemic analgesia. (Note: Topical anesthetics were strictly NOT prescribed for home use due to corneal toxicity risk).
Positive Behavioral Change: To promote rapid epithelial healing and prevent recurrent erosions, the patient must apply the thick ointment directly into the lower fornix right before attempting to sleep. The immediate, soothing lubrication reduces the painful friction of the eyelid over the raw nerve endings, providing instant positive reinforcement and ensuring compliance with the prophylactic antibiotic regimen while they rest.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP or Optometrist review in 24-48 hours to confirm reduction in defect size and exclude secondary infection.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient experiences a sudden worsening of vision, the appearance of a white/grey spot on the colored part of the eye, or if the severe pain suddenly returns and worsens despite systemic painkillers.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a superficial scratch; requires follow-up to ensure it does not become infected.
Clinical Course: The intense pain should begin to subside significantly within 24 hours as the rapidly dividing surface cells heal over the defect. Total resolution is expected within 48-72 hours.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not wear contact lenses. If your vision suddenly drops, or you see a new white spot developing on your eye, you must proceed to the emergency department immediately.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The fingernail scraped off the top protective layer of your eye, exposing raw nerves. The antibiotic ointment acts as a physical bandage to stop your eyelid rubbing it, while preventing bacteria from entering the open scratch.
Actionable Steps: Take the oral painkillers strictly by the clock. Apply the thick ointment right before sleep. Keep the eye closed and rested in a dimly lit room as much as possible tonight.

